WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Before choosing protections, identify what data is sensitive.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:19.960
Connection strings, API keys, certificates, signing keys, access tokens, refresh tokens, service credentials, personal data, customer records, operational details, and administrative actions may all need different controls.

3
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:26.620
Sensitivity should influence storage, transmission, logging, retention, and access review.

4
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.460
Secrets should be protected outside ordinary source and configuration files.

5
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:49.160
Local development, staging, and production may use different mechanisms, but the principle is the same: secrets need controlled access, rotation, auditing, and a way to avoid accidental disclosure through repositories, build logs, crash dumps, screenshots, telemetry, or support exports.

6
00:00:49.440 --> 00:01:01.840
ASP.NET Core Data Protection is important because framework-managed features may rely on it to protect cookies, antiforgery tokens, password reset tokens, and other protected payloads.

7
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:16.420
Teams do not need to memorize the internals to use it responsibly, but they should understand key storage, key rotation, deployment topology, and backup implications at a high level, especially when applications run across multiple instances.

8
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.460
Avoid custom cryptography.

9
00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:26.480
Encryption, signing, hashing, token protection, and key management are specialized areas where small mistakes can last a long time.

10
00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:32.980
Use supported framework features, platform services, identity provider mechanisms, and approved libraries.

11
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:40.440
When a requirement is unusual, seek specialist review rather than inventing a format in application code.

12
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:50.960
Sensitive data should not appear in logs, exception pages, telemetry, analytics events, screenshots, support bundles, or user-visible validation messages.

13
00:01:51.060 --> 00:02:01.540
Structured logging and monitoring are valuable, but they should capture useful context without recording secret values, raw tokens, private records, or unnecessary personal data.

14
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:05.420
Data minimization and retention are defensive practices.

15
00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.760
If an application does not need to collect, store, or display sensitive data, avoid doing so.

16
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.020
If it does need the data, protect it according to its value and lifecycle.

17
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:23.000
Security is easier when the system keeps less high-risk information and has clear ownership for what remains.
